      <!-- SECTION 1: THE CLINICAL SCIENCE OF COLOR VISION -->
      <article class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase font-semibold">
          Retinal Photobiology & Chromatic Pathways
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-2xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          What is Color Vision Deficiency (CVD)?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Color vision deficiency (CVD)</strong>, colloquially known as <strong>color blindness</strong> or <em>daltonism</em>, is a common sensory condition characterized by an impaired ability to distinguish between certain shades in the visible spectrum. The term <em>daltonism</em> honors English chemist John Dalton, who published the first scientific treatise on color blindness in 1794 after discovering that he and his brother perceived ripe cherries and green leaves as indistinguishable. Contrary to common misconceptions, individuals with color blindness rarely perceive the world in pure grayscale; rather, their chromatic gamut is shifted, compressed, or missing key wavelength discrimination channels.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          Human color perception depends on specialized photoreceptor cells situated in the neurosensory retina: approximately <strong>120 million rods</strong> (mediating scotopic nocturnal vision and achromatic luminance) and <strong>6 to 7 million cones</strong> (concentrated in the central <em>fovea centralis</em> for high-acuity photopic color perception). In individuals with <strong>normal trichromacy</strong>, color vision is mediated by three discrete cone photopigments, each calibrated to a characteristic spectral absorbance peak. When any of these cone photopigments undergo genetic mutation or are absent, a standardized <strong>color deficiency test</strong> or <strong>color blindness test</strong> is required to quantify the exact photopic defect:
        </p>

        <!-- 3 Cone Classes Card Grid -->
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 pt-2">
          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
            <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-rose-100 dark:bg-rose-950 text-rose-600 dark:text-rose-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-4 text-base font-mono">
              L-Cone
            </div>
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-2">Erythrolabe (Long Wavelength)</h3>
            <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono text-rose-600 dark:text-rose-400 font-bold mb-3">Peak Absorption: ~560 nm (Red)</div>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
              Encoded by the <em>OPN1LW</em> gene on the X-chromosome. Spectral shifts or deletions cause Protanomaly (red weakness) or Protanopia (red blindness).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
            <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-emerald-100 dark:bg-emerald-950 text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-4 text-base font-mono">
              M-Cone
            </div>
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-2">Chlorolabe (Medium Wavelength)</h3>
            <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-bold mb-3">Peak Absorption: ~530 nm (Green)</div>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
              Encoded by the <em>OPN1MW</em> gene array on the X-chromosome. Deficits cause Deuteranomaly (green weakness) or Deuteranopia (green blindness)—representing ~75% of all CVD cases.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
            <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-2xl bg-blue-100 dark:bg-blue-950 text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold mb-4 text-base font-mono">
              S-Cone
            </div>
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white mb-2">Cyanolabe (Short Wavelength)</h3>
            <div class="text-xs sm:text-sm font-mono text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 font-bold mb-3">Peak Absorption: ~420 nm (Blue)</div>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400 leading-relaxed">
              Encoded by the <em>OPN1SW</em> gene on autosomal chromosome 7. Mutations trigger Tritanomaly or Tritanopia (blue-yellow confusion), affecting biological males and females equally.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

