      <!-- SECTION 3: DR. ISHIHARA'S BREAKTHROUGH (1917) -->
      <article class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase font-semibold">
          Historical Psychometrics & Plate Functional Categories
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-2xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Dr. Shinobu Ishihara’s Breakthrough (1917)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          In 1917, Dr. Shinobu Ishihara, a military ophthalmologist and professor at the University of Tokyo, published his pioneering pseudo-isochromatic plate series to screen recruits for the Imperial Japanese Army. His clinical genius lay in the realization that individuals with color vision defects could not simply be tested with uniform color swatches, because variations in brightness and saturation allow clever observers to guess hues.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          Ishihara systematically calibrated <strong>4 functional plate categories</strong> to isolate specific retinal cone responses:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 pt-2">
          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">1. Demonstration Plates</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
              High-contrast plates (e.g. Plate 1, showing "9" or "12") designed to be seen clearly by individuals with normal trichromacy, anomalous trichromacy, and complete dichromacy alike. Used exclusively to calibrate user comprehension and detect malingering.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">2. Transformation Plates</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
              Plates where normal observers read one number (e.g., "8" or "5"), while colorblind observers read an entirely different number (e.g., "3" or "2"). The numeral incorporates both chromatic and luminance contours that split observers based on cone sensitivity.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">3. Vanishing Plates</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
              Plates featuring numbers embedded against an iso-luminant background along red-green confusion lines. Normal trichromats immediately see the embedded digit, whereas red-green deficient individuals perceive only a chaotic field of dots.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">4. Hidden Digit Plates (Reverse Plates)</h3>
            <p class="text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
              Plates where normal trichromats see nothing or random noise, but colorblind individuals clearly read a hidden numeral. Normal observers are visually camouflaged by chromatic noise, while colorblind observers perceive subtle luminance groupings.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>

      <!-- SECTION 4: THE GENETICS OF COLOR BLINDNESS -->
      <article class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase font-semibold">
          X-Linked Recessive Inheritance & Opsin Genomics
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-2xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          The Genetics of Color Blindness: Why Men Are Affected More
        </h2>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          The pronounced epidemiological skew in color blindness—affecting approximately <strong>1 in 12 men (8.0%)</strong> compared to only <strong>1 in 200 women (0.5%)</strong>—is directly explained by Mendelian genetics and X-linked recessive inheritance.
        </p>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed">
          The genes responsible for synthesizing red photopigments (<em>OPN1LW</em>) and green photopigments (<em>OPN1MW</em>) are situated directly adjacent to one another on the long arm of the X-chromosome (locus <strong>Xq28</strong>). Because these two genes share 98% nucleotide sequence identity, unequal homologous crossing-over frequently occurs during maternal meiosis, creating chimeric gene deletions or hybrid amino acid substitutions.
        </p>

        <div class="p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900/60 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 space-y-4">
          <h3 class="font-heading font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">
            How Color Blindness Passes Through Generations
          </h3>
          <ul class="space-y-3 text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside">
            <li>
              <strong>Hemizygous Males (XY):</strong> Because men possess only a single X-chromosome (inherited from their mother), any mutation in the maternal <em>OPN1LW</em> or <em>OPN1MW</em> gene manifests directly as clinical color blindness. Males cannot be asymptomatic carriers.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Carrier Females:</strong> Women possess two X-chromosomes. If one X-chromosome carries a mutated allele and the other carries a normal wild-type allele, the dominant healthy gene ensures normal cone synthesis. These women have normal vision but have a <strong>50% probability of passing the colorblind gene</strong> to their sons.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Colorblind Daughters:</strong> For a female to be colorblind, she must inherit mutated X-chromosomes from <em>both</em> parents—meaning her biological father <em>must</em> be colorblind, and her biological mother must be at least a carrier.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </article>

