      <!-- SECTION 7: FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS ACCORDION -->
      <article class="space-y-6">
        <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3.5 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-900 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 font-mono text-xs sm:text-sm uppercase font-semibold">
          Featured Snippets & Frequently Asked Questions
        </div>
        <h2 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-2xl sm:text-4xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Frequently Asked Questions About Color Blindness Tests
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <!-- Q1 -->
          <details class="group p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 transition-all">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-heading font-bold text-lg text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">
              <span>Can you fail an online color blind test if your screen brightness is wrong?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0 faq-chevron" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pt-4">
              Yes. Low screen brightness, severe ambient glare, or active blue-light filters (such as Apple TrueTone or Night Shift) alter the chromatic saturation of pseudo-isochromatic dots. This can cause anomalous trichromats or even normal observers to miss subtle vanishing plates. Always calibrate your display to 100% brightness and standard daylight color temperature before testing.
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- Q2 -->
          <details class="group p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 transition-all">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-heading font-bold text-lg text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">
              <span>Is color blindness curable or reversible?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0 faq-chevron" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pt-4">
              Congenital color blindness is caused by genetic mutations in cone opsin genes and currently has no approved medical cure in humans. However, groundbreaking recombinant adeno-associated virus (rAAV) gene therapy clinical trials have successfully restored trichromatic color vision in adult dichromatic squirrel monkeys, offering strong future therapeutic promise for human ocular gene editing.
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- Q3 -->
          <details class="group p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 transition-all">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-heading font-bold text-lg text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">
              <span>Do color blind glasses (EnChroma or Pilestone) cure color blindness?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0 faq-chevron" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pt-4">
              No. Color blind glasses do not cure color blindness and cannot generate photoreceptors that do not exist. They utilize multi-notch dielectric optical coatings that filter out overlapping wavelength bands between red and green photoreceptors. This boosts chromatic contrast for anomalous trichromats (mild Deuteranomaly/Protanomaly), but does not benefit dichromats who completely lack an M-cone or L-cone. They are prohibited during statutory employment physicals.
            </div>
          </details>

          <!-- Q4 -->
          <details class="group p-6 rounded-3xl bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 transition-all">
            <summary class="flex items-center justify-between cursor-pointer list-none font-heading font-bold text-lg text-zinc-950 dark:text-white">
              <span>Why are men significantly more prone to color blindness than women?</span>
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-zinc-400 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-200 shrink-0 faq-chevron" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M19 9l-7 7-7-7" />
              </svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="mt-4 text-base text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300 leading-relaxed border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pt-4">
              The genes for red and green cone opsins (OPN1LW and OPN1MW) reside on the X-chromosome. Biological males possess only one X-chromosome (XY), inherited from their mother. If that single chromosome carries a mutated gene, the male will express color blindness (8% prevalence). Biological females possess two X-chromosomes (XX); a mutated gene on one is compensated by the normal gene on the other, making females asymptomatic carriers (only 0.5% express CVD).
            </div>
